{
	title: 'Bash lifecycle as in the old Hal: timeout, kill on exit',
	spec: "Bash commands never outlive the Hal that ran them, as in the old Hal (questions.md items 28-29, 32): every clean host exit (quit, Ctrl-R restart, SIGTERM or SIGHUP, giving up the host role) aborts running turns, which kills their bash process groups (SIGTERM, SIGKILL 2 s later), not only when the last Hal process quits; the next host continues those turns as interrupted. Only a hard crash or kill -9 can leave orphans. The bash tool takes an optional timeout in ms (default 120000) after which the whole group is killed and the result is the output so far plus 'timed out after Ns', so a background child holding stdout ('cmd &') ends the call within the timeout. Approval treats a word like v1.2 (v followed by a digit) as a version, not a path, so 'git checkout v1.2' needs no approval.",
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Kill on exit is host.init\'s exit handler aborting every running turn, no longer only when pauseOnExit. history.stop runs first and clears history.state.running, so the abort records nothing; the next host continues as interrupted. There is no in-process "give up host role": server.stop is only used by tests, so every hand-over is an exit.',
		"The exit handler is synchronous: bash's close never fires, but tools.killGroup SIGTERMs synchronously and its detached sh still sends SIGKILL 2 s later. A headless process (no terminal handlers) still leaves turns on SIGTERM; only the terminal path maps SIGTERM/SIGHUP to exit.",
		'Timeout: input.timeout in ms (non-positive or non-numeric falls back to 120000); the status line is `[timed out after Ns]` with the output so far. The e2e test (Ctrl-R with a peer, sleep must die, turn ends completed) fails with the old pauseOnExit guard.',
		'Approval: the checkout/restore path lookahead skips tokens starting v<digit>, so `git checkout v1.2` is free but `git checkout v1.2 -- a.ts` still asks.'
	],
	needs: ['1r', 'xz', '6f'],
	foldInto: ['1r', 'xz', '6f']
}
